{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-surreal-squeeze","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Proof","doco:Section"],"label":"prf:surreal-squeeze","kind":"proof","title":"Proof of surreal squeeze","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/lem-surreal-squeeze"],"text":"surreal-squeeze-proof.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prf:surreal-squeeze\"></a><p>Multiply both sides by the strictly positive element\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varepsilon</code> to obtain the equivalent inequality</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">\\delta^2 > \\varepsilon^4.</code></pre>\n<p>Since <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varepsilon</code> is a positive infinitesimal\n(<a href=\"#def:reeb-epsilon\" data-at=\"stochastic-mechanics/classical-limit-construction\">Reeb infinitesimal</a>, positivity and archimedean\nsmallness), every positive integer multiple <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n \\cdot \\varepsilon^4</code> is\nstill bounded above by any standard real.  In particular\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\varepsilon^4 &#x3C; \\delta^2</code>.  <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\square</code></p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/appendix-surreals/surreal-squeeze-proof.md","heading":"Proof","page":"b/prf-surreal-squeeze","qa":{"counts":{"pass":59,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":15,"unknown":0,"stale":58},"milnor":{"total":2,"notPassing":0}}}
